WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.040
In the first half of this talk I'm going to try to slip two plots by you that

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:15.960
show some sort of problematic data quality issue. So if when I do that you

00:00:15.960 --> 00:00:21.600
you happen to notice that, just act like you've fallen asleep and so that way if

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.799
everybody looks like they've fallen asleep I know that the talk is engaging.

00:00:30.879 --> 00:00:38.720
So a year or so ago we were working on an agent for data analysis and it was

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:44.160
sort of cloud code shaped but it also had the ability to run Python code. And

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:48.000
we wanted to make sure that when the agent made plots it could see the plots.

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:55.759
So I load in the tips data set and I say please plot the total bill versus the tip.

00:00:55.759 --> 00:01:00.680
So when people tip at a restaurant they tend to calculate it as a percentage so

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:06.519
we would expect that as the total bill increases the tip increases as well. So a

00:01:06.519 --> 00:01:12.080
positive relationship. So I send this message to the agent that I'm working on.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.199
It runs some Python code, it returns a plot, and it says there's a strong

00:01:17.199 --> 00:01:25.080
positive association. Okay nobody's acting like they're asleep so that's

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:31.400
good for engagement I think. There's an issue here which is that the plot is

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:38.320
showing that as the total bill increases the tip seems to decrease. So I order a

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.959
$2 drink and the tip is $10 for some reason and a $50 meal and for some

00:01:43.959 --> 00:01:52.760
reason folks are tipping $2. This is weird and this is because I added a

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:56.239
little trick before I asked the agent to make that plot. I wanted to make sure

00:01:56.239 --> 00:02:02.000
it could actually see the plots so I took the value of the total bill and

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.040
subtracted that from the maximum value so that every time I plot the total bill

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:11.160
against any other variable inside of the data set I would see the opposite of the

00:02:11.160 --> 00:02:16.320
relationship that I would expect to see. And I wanted to test the vision of this

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:20.639
agent so I assumed if the agent can actually see the plot it's going to tell

00:02:20.639 --> 00:02:25.919
me that oh that's weird for some reason the relationship is the opposite of what

00:02:25.919 --> 00:02:34.399
I expected. And yet for some reason it didn't do so. So we were looking at the

00:02:34.399 --> 00:02:38.399
API documentation again and again like oh maybe this JSON field needs to be

00:02:38.399 --> 00:02:45.080
pushed inside of an element or something like that or I've used the wrong base 64

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:48.440
encoding and if I switch to a different library it'll work. So we try all these

00:02:48.520 --> 00:02:55.039
things and it's not working and out of desperation we try one more approach. We

00:02:55.039 --> 00:03:00.759
say run this code, only this code, nothing but this code, tell me how many points

00:03:00.759 --> 00:03:07.360
there are and what color they are. This is some seaborn code. I'm taking a number

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:15.520
between 2 and 10. I'm plotting that many points on a Cartesian plane and I'm

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:22.320
assigning those points a random color. So I'm sampling an RG and a B and every

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:26.800
time the agent runs that code because we don't know the state of the random

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:32.520
number generator the number of points in the color will be different. And so in

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:35.679
order to be able to tell me how many points there are and what color they are

00:03:35.679 --> 00:03:44.360
the agent has to actually be able to see the plot. So I say run this code, only

00:03:44.399 --> 00:03:48.919
this code, nothing but this code. The agent runs the code. It sees there are

00:03:48.919 --> 00:03:59.919
three cyan points and it says so. I thought it wasn't working. We had tried

00:03:59.919 --> 00:04:03.720
all this stuff of hitting the APIs in different ways thinking that we were

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:07.839
making some mistake but it turns out that the agents actually for some

00:04:07.839 --> 00:04:11.800
reason can see the plots just fine but in this case where their priors are

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:18.279
being controverted they refuse to say so. So my colleague Sarah and I put this

00:04:18.279 --> 00:04:24.399
blog post together called when plotting LLM see what they expect to see and the

00:04:24.399 --> 00:04:30.320
evidence we use in that blog post is an LLM evaluation called BluffBench.

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:35.920
BluffBench puts these models in a bunch of situations like this where axis

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.160
labels might indicate to the agent that they should expect to see some

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:45.839
relationship like as the total bill increases the tip should increase but

00:04:45.839 --> 00:04:52.559
then the evidence shown in the plot is actually the opposite of that. At the

00:04:52.559 --> 00:04:59.640
time the frontier models of the time did terribly on this so GPT-5, GeminiPro

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:08.239
2.5 and CloudSonic 4.5 were the leading models from the major labs. We tried so

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.040
much stuff short of saying by the way this plot is going to contradict your

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:16.839
priors tell me what's actually in it to try to drive these scores up and we

00:05:16.839 --> 00:05:22.279
really struggled to do so. Hilariously we enabled thinking and then looked at the

00:05:22.279 --> 00:05:26.839
thinking traces afterward and the model would say huh that doesn't look like I

00:05:26.839 --> 00:05:32.880
expected it to. It would close the thinking tag and then say looks like I

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:42.959
expected. So of course the best way to drive the score up on an LLM evaluation

00:05:42.959 --> 00:05:50.440
is to wait six months and the plot looks a little bit like this now. So with

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:57.399
with thinking disabled the scores sort of bunch around 25% and over time they

00:05:57.399 --> 00:06:02.519
tend to get a little bit better. The leading models of the day on on this

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:07.279
evaluation which are CloudFable 5 and Gemini 3 flash tend to be in the

00:06:07.279 --> 00:06:16.160
ballpark of like 70% on this. Notably this is subhuman. I would guess that

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:19.640
anybody in the audience here would score around 100% on these. These are not

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:23.600
subtle contradictions of your priors. This is literally like we're flipping

00:06:24.399 --> 00:06:32.959
the axis on each plot. And so from this we learn that models largely fail to

00:06:32.959 --> 00:06:37.799
override their own priors about data when they're presented with evidence

00:06:37.799 --> 00:06:49.880
that contradicts. There are all sorts of issues with BluffBench. The the

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:54.640
evaluation only presents the sorts of information and tools that the model

00:06:54.640 --> 00:07:00.959
needs to sort of activate the prior and then see whether the agent will

00:07:01.119 --> 00:07:06.440
contradict it or not. And so since then we've wondered what if we put these

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:11.239
models in a data analysis situation that looks a lot more like real world data

00:07:11.239 --> 00:07:15.399
analysis. So we give it a bunch of stuff that doesn't matter. Like you put it in

00:07:15.399 --> 00:07:21.200
a harness that looks like cloud code or something like that and you tell it to

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:25.119
load in some data and you have a bunch of back and forth until eventually you

00:07:25.119 --> 00:07:31.559
ask it to make some plot and that plot has some sort of very subtle artifact

00:07:31.559 --> 00:07:35.920
inside of the visualization that could feasibly result from a real data

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:42.239
generating process. So for example let's assume that I receive some patient data

00:07:42.279 --> 00:07:48.239
and I'm just making some exploratory plots to understand what's going on. I

00:07:48.239 --> 00:07:54.480
say could you plot BMI versus cholesterol. The agent runs some code and

00:07:54.519 --> 00:08:00.320
it generates a plot that shows that the BMI tends to well as the BMI increases

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:07.079
the cholesterol tends to increase. Okay nobody's sleeping so it seems like the

00:08:07.119 --> 00:08:14.559
audience is engaged. There's something a little weird going on here. I'll zoom in

00:08:14.559 --> 00:08:19.640
on this a little bit. As the BMI increases the cholesterol tends to

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:24.679
increase except that in the middle sort of right along where you would expect

00:08:24.839 --> 00:08:31.880
the fitted line to land there are a bunch of points. And so if we take a step

00:08:31.880 --> 00:08:35.440
back and we're like that's weird what's going on. Turns out if you filter down to

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.359
one of the clinics that contributed data they didn't include cholesterol and

00:08:39.359 --> 00:08:45.440
the data model imputed the cholesterol value for those points. So if I were to

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:51.400
then go and do data analysis on this data set without realizing this I would

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:55.440
make a bunch of errors. And so I either need to filter those out or change them

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.880
to missing values or use measures that meaningfully incorporate the fact that

00:09:00.880 --> 00:09:07.080
some of these points are synthetic. It turns out that even today's frontier

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:13.280
models do terribly on this sort of thing. So we designed a follow up LLM

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:17.479
evaluation called Bluff Bench 2 that is very similar to Bluff Bench except that

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:22.919
it feels a lot more like real world data analysis. And even those same leaders

00:09:22.919 --> 00:09:28.559
Cloud Fable 5 and Gemini 3 Flash are sort of in the mid teens in terms of the

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:33.840
percent of cases where they actually notice that something is awry in the moment

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:41.119
that they need to. So from the second evaluation we learned something a little

00:09:41.119 --> 00:09:45.760
bit more general which is that models fail to notice subtle data quality issues

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:52.719
that most data scientists would not miss. One might think at this point should we

00:09:52.760 --> 00:09:57.159
burn it all down. Should we be using LLMs for data analysis at all. I'll hand it

00:09:57.159 --> 00:09:59.359
off to my colleague Sarah to answer that question.

00:10:09.799 --> 00:10:15.880
So as Simon has just explained LLMs can fail at these tasks that are really

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:23.320
central to data analysis. Bluff Bench 1 the first Bluff Bench is evidence that

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:28.799
models can fail to override their priors about data. And then we also saw in the

00:10:28.799 --> 00:10:34.599
second eval that they can also fail to notice subtle data quality issues. And

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:38.760
both of these abilities like the ability to not let your expectations interfere

00:10:39.479 --> 00:10:44.520
with your ability to see what's right in front of you and the ability to use a

00:10:44.520 --> 00:10:49.919
plot or something else to notice data quality issues are really central to data

00:10:49.919 --> 00:10:54.520
analysis. These are things that we would want any person doing any kind of data

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:59.119
science to be able to do. And that also applies to agents.

00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:08.400
And there's another problem which is that LLMs are really great performers. You

00:11:08.400 --> 00:11:13.599
know they can put on this really convincing act of correctness. So we saw

00:11:13.599 --> 00:11:19.000
in Bluff Bench the model will readily interpret these plots and it acts very

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:24.200
confident about what it's saying. And it's very easy to sort of fall into this

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:29.119
pattern of trust with them. The only problem is that like in this plot where

00:11:29.119 --> 00:11:34.479
we have a clearly positive trend that the model has described as a clear

00:11:34.479 --> 00:11:38.880
negative relationship is that it's like this really convincing performance right.

00:11:38.919 --> 00:11:45.719
But it's one that occasionally fails to match reality. And these answers all look

00:11:45.719 --> 00:11:50.359
the same. They're really hard. It's really hard to distinguish incorrect LLM

00:11:50.359 --> 00:11:55.719
responses from correct ones. So here's another plot from Bluff Bench where I

00:11:55.719 --> 00:11:59.320
think any person would say you know this looks like a U. It's a parabolic

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:05.000
relationship. And then three responses from the same model. It's a little hard

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:08.919
to read them but like they all have the same tone. They have the same length. The

00:12:08.919 --> 00:12:13.840
model is equally confident in all of those. And you would have to look at the

00:12:13.840 --> 00:12:21.280
plot to know that the first two are very wrong and the middle one is correct. So

00:12:21.280 --> 00:12:26.400
it's not just that the model is making mistakes but that the answers look the

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:33.039
same. It can be very hard to distinguish the correct answers from the wrong ones

00:12:33.039 --> 00:12:41.440
unless you already know what the data looks like. OK. So again we see the LLMs

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:47.039
can fail at tasks that are central to data analysis. And not only do they fail

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:51.679
they can cloak these incorrect answers in confident descriptions and it makes

00:12:51.679 --> 00:12:57.880
it really hard to tell what's correct and what's not. That sounds a bit

00:12:57.880 --> 00:13:04.479
negative. I'm going to say that LLMs are still useful for data analysis and we

00:13:04.479 --> 00:13:08.400
don't need to throw this whole endeavor away just because of evidence like Bluff

00:13:08.440 --> 00:13:14.239
Bench and Bluff Bench 2. It doesn't mean the LLMs are useless anywhere that

00:13:14.239 --> 00:13:20.599
you're doing anything that you value correctness. Instead the task is really

00:13:20.599 --> 00:13:25.119
to figure out how they can fit into the work and to design around their

00:13:25.119 --> 00:13:34.880
limitations. So I said I'm going to say that LLMs are still useful for data

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:39.880
analysis but we're going to need to do two things in these agents to make them

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:45.200
actually work for us. And the first is to make it as easy as possible for them

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:50.080
to be right. And then the second is that when they inevitably make mistakes to

00:13:50.080 --> 00:13:54.679
make it matter less when they're wrong. It's going to prevent mistakes but then

00:13:54.679 --> 00:14:01.520
mitigate them when they probably eventually happen. This whole time we've

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:05.719
been talking about agents but just really quickly all I mean when I say

00:14:05.719 --> 00:14:10.239
agent is just a model hooked up in a way so that it can gather information from

00:14:10.239 --> 00:14:15.000
the world like reading files and then also can alter the world so it can run

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:25.679
code or it can edit your files. And you might already use an agent but you can

00:14:25.679 --> 00:14:29.679
also build them yourself and these lessons are probably most relevant if

00:14:29.679 --> 00:14:32.960
you're building something yourself or you're altering something that already

00:14:32.960 --> 00:14:40.840
exists. At Posit we have an agent called Posit Assistant which is both a general

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:46.559
purpose coding agent for R and Python and also a specialized data analysis

00:14:46.559 --> 00:14:52.960
agent. And it lives in our IDEs, Positron and RStudio. And I'm just going to use

00:14:52.960 --> 00:15:01.000
this as an example for the rest of the talk. Okay. So I said even though they

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:05.320
make all these mistakes and like they seem, you know, these mistakes we see in

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:09.159
BluffBench are like kind of bad. You know, these are not things that we want a

00:15:09.159 --> 00:15:12.960
human agent to do. They're not things we want an LLM agent to do. But LLMs still

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:17.840
have a use for data analysis. They can actually be very useful. But we need to

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:25.479
make it as easy as possible for them to be right. And what this task looks like

00:15:25.479 --> 00:15:29.080
is constructing the environment, everything that goes around the model, so

00:15:29.080 --> 00:15:33.719
that it's as easy as possible for the model to sort of fall into correctness. We

00:15:33.719 --> 00:15:38.559
don't want it to have to work very hard to, you know, be right. We want it to just

00:15:38.559 --> 00:15:45.320
fall into this path of correctness. And I'm saying build the environment around

00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:49.640
the model. Because typically you control the harness, all of this stuff around the

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:54.200
model, like the prompting and the tools and the skills. And you're not actually

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:58.400
controlling the model itself. Unless, you know, you're one of these labs that makes

00:15:58.400 --> 00:16:07.280
both models and the harness. I'm going to talk about two things to construct this

00:16:07.280 --> 00:16:13.159
ecosystem around the model that makes it easy to be right. The first is that code

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:18.239
should really be the foundation of what the agent is doing. So, you want to have

00:16:18.239 --> 00:16:23.799
the model write code. And then the second is to design the harness with correctness

00:16:23.799 --> 00:16:32.400
in mind. Okay. So, when you're making an agent, I think if you want it to be

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:37.159
correct, it is really helpful to lean on code. And there's a couple reasons for

00:16:37.159 --> 00:16:42.640
this. The first is that I think by now many people are familiar with this. But

00:16:42.799 --> 00:16:49.119
LLMs are actually great at writing code. This is really one of their strengths. And

00:16:49.119 --> 00:16:54.479
then the second is that, you know, from years of everyone doing data analysis as

00:16:54.479 --> 00:16:59.159
humans, we've learned that code is great for data analysis. It's reproducible,

00:16:59.400 --> 00:17:05.280
it's transparent, and it's auditable. And the same things that are true for humans

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:09.680
doing data analysis is true for LLMs doing analysis. We shouldn't, you know,

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:15.000
abandon these learnings of code just because there's a new tool. So, we want

00:17:15.000 --> 00:17:21.920
the agent to write code. And then we also want to design the harness with

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:28.319
correctness in mind. So, for Positive Assistant, we have a lot of prompting

00:17:28.319 --> 00:17:32.680
that encourages it to prioritize correctness over progress. If you let

00:17:32.680 --> 00:17:36.160
models sort of go on their own, they often will just kind of advance the

00:17:36.160 --> 00:17:42.119
analysis forward instead of thinking about correctness, subtlety, statistical

00:17:42.119 --> 00:17:49.560
rigor. So, we have a lot of prompting encouraging it to follow this path. The

00:17:49.560 --> 00:17:53.439
second is having tools that serve the same purposes of correctness. So, these

00:17:53.439 --> 00:17:57.280
are things, again, having code is the foundation of what your agent does. Have

00:17:57.280 --> 00:18:03.119
the agent run code. You could imagine an agent that, you know, does not run code.

00:18:03.119 --> 00:18:06.839
You just hand it a CSV, and it computes, you know, in its head the median of

00:18:06.839 --> 00:18:11.079
columns or the number of rows. Generally, it's going to be a lot less successful

00:18:11.079 --> 00:18:15.160
than having it run a bit of code, see that result, and then give you the answer.

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:23.400
And then you also want the agent to have enough access to files and environment

00:18:23.400 --> 00:18:28.400
and data so that it has a, you know, good chance of building enough context to

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:32.520
actually get the answer right. There's often so much nuance in data analysis,

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:37.719
and a lot of that comes from, you know, not just the data, but all of the context

00:18:37.719 --> 00:18:46.599
around that data. And you want the agent to have access to that. Okay. And it

00:18:46.599 --> 00:18:50.760
turns out that performance improves when the environment makes it easy to be

00:18:50.760 --> 00:18:58.199
right. So, we ran an evaluation on positive assistant on the bluff bench

00:18:58.199 --> 00:19:03.800
tasks and compared it to the result from the bluff bench package that just has a

00:19:03.800 --> 00:19:08.680
minimal harness in place. All the model can really do there is make a plot. And

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:12.280
it turns out that when you have all this stuff wrapped around the model, pushing

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:17.800
it towards this path of correctness, performance improves. So, generally,

00:19:17.800 --> 00:19:22.040
positive assistant has done better on this evaluation than just the minimal

00:19:22.040 --> 00:19:31.319
harness present in bluff bench. Okay. So, even with all that said, the agent is

00:19:31.319 --> 00:19:35.560
probably still going to make mistakes. You know, humans make mistakes. Models

00:19:35.560 --> 00:19:43.160
make mistakes. And so, we need to make it matter less when they're wrong. Okay.

00:19:43.160 --> 00:19:47.880
And again, we get a lot of this mitigation from just using code as the

00:19:47.880 --> 00:19:54.119
foundation of the agent. One thing code is great for is that it makes auditing

00:19:54.119 --> 00:19:58.280
what happened in the analysis easy. And for positive assistant, we didn't just

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:02.599
want the model to write code. Somewhere we wanted the person to be able to see

00:20:02.599 --> 00:20:08.199
that code and have a chance to audit it and make catch mistakes. This doesn't

00:20:08.199 --> 00:20:12.599
mean that, you know, you need to comb through it every time. But it's there so

00:20:12.599 --> 00:20:16.040
you can inspect it if you suspect something has gone wrong and have a

00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:20.520
chance at catching a mistake and then diagnosing it. We've also done things

00:20:20.520 --> 00:20:24.839
like make it easy to read with syntax highlighting and the formatting just so

00:20:24.839 --> 00:20:35.000
this auditing process is more likely to happen. And then positive assistant

00:20:35.000 --> 00:20:40.119
also has a shared environment between the user and the agent. So, you can see

00:20:40.119 --> 00:20:45.400
eye to eye with the agent. You see the same outputs and run the same code. And

00:20:45.479 --> 00:20:48.839
so, the model isn't guessing, you know, what data you have loaded or other

00:20:48.839 --> 00:20:52.599
things that are in your environment. It's operating in the same environment

00:20:52.599 --> 00:20:58.920
that you as the user are. And this turns out to be very useful, especially for

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:01.880
something like data analysis where it can be very iterative and you're turning

00:21:01.880 --> 00:21:06.119
the same object, you know, over and over making small changes. You want to have

00:21:06.119 --> 00:21:18.119
a shared environment with the agent. Okay. So, in the first half of this talk,

00:21:18.119 --> 00:21:22.839
we said that LLMs can fail at these tasks that are central to data analysis.

00:21:25.079 --> 00:21:29.319
But they're still useful for data analysis. Especially if you do a couple

00:21:29.319 --> 00:21:33.880
things. One is focus on making it easy for them to be right. You know, as easy

00:21:33.880 --> 00:21:38.839
as possible. So, they just fall into this path of correctness. And the second

